# Steerability: can the criterion be changed at query time? (28 cases in 14 flipped pairs.)
#
# THE CLAIM UNDER TEST. A vendor's marketing says a cross-encoder reranker "cannot be
# steered" while a decision model can. That is a comparative claim about a capability, and
# this file is built so it can be checked rather than believed.
#
# THE DESIGN. Every pair holds the DOCUMENT and the TOPIC fixed and changes only the
# CRITERION, so that the correct answer flips. The metric is not per-case agreement, it is
# **pair accuracy**: both sides of a pair right, or the pair does not count.
#
# WHY THAT METRIC SETTLES SOMETHING. A scorer whose inputs are only (query, document) is a
# function of those two things. Hold both fixed and its output cannot move, so it answers
# both sides of a pair identically and scores **0 % pair accuracy by construction**, not by
# experiment. That bound applies to a classic cross-encoder and to embedding similarity.
#
# IT DOES NOT APPLY TO AN INSTRUCTION-FOLLOWING RERANKER, AND THE MARKETING CLAIM IS FALSE.
# We checked before publishing this file. Voyage sells `rerank-2.5` and `rerank-2.5-lite` as
# instruction-following rerankers, with the instruction appended to the query in natural
# language, and their own worked example is our pitch almost word for word ("retrieve
# regulatory documents and legal statutes, not court cases"). ZeroEntropy's `zerank-2` takes
# instructions and business context. Contextual AI shipped one in March 2025 that handles
# recency, document type and source priority. Four benchmarks exist to measure the
# capability (MAIR, IFIR, FollowIR, InstructIR). All of them are cheaper per token than the
# model behind this bench.
#
# So this file does NOT show that only a decision model can be steered. It shows that this
# one can, it says where it fails, and the fair baseline - an instruction-following reranker
# run over the same pairs - has not been run. Until it is, nothing here supports a pitch.
# The one place the literature still leaves open is exclusion: models solve at most one
# ExcluIR query in eight (arXiv 2502.13506, SIGIR 2025), and five of the fourteen criteria
# below are exclusions. That is a hypothesis this bench is too small to settle, not a result.
# Read `docs/results/2026-09-24-steerability/summary.md` before quoting any of this.
#
# TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS, PREDICTED TO FAIL BEFORE THE RUN (st-13, st-14). Their criterion
# turns on a date comparison, which is this model class's declared weakness (paper, 5.3). If
# they pass, that weakness is narrower than the model card says; if they fail, the bench is
# measuring what it claims to measure. Either outcome is informative, which is the point of
# including them.
#
# Single-annotator, authored 2026-09-24. Content is Spanish without accents. Point name
# `steerability` so these cases never mix with the page-triage numbers of `core.jsonl`.
{"id": "st-01a", "pair": "st-01", "point": "steerability", "input": {"purpose": "cifras de viviendas danadas por el sismo del 12 de marzo", "title": "El Diario del Istmo: mas de mil viviendas afectadas por el sismo", "url": "https://eldiariodelistmo.test/sismo-viviendas", "text": "El Diario del Istmo. Juchitan, 15 de marzo de 2026. Las autoridades reportaron 1.240 viviendas con danos tras el sismo del jueves, de las cuales 312 presentan dano estructural severo. La cifra procede del censo preliminar de Proteccion Civil."}, "expected": "keep"}
